(function(){
COURSE_UNITS.push({"id":"mobility-commerce","num":"16","title":"Build the Giants: Mobility & Commerce","color":"violet","lessons":[{"id":"uber-api-layer","title":"How Uber’s API layer evolved"},{"id":"airbnb-soa","title":"From Monorail to services at Airbnb"},{"id":"ecommerce-workflow","title":"From cart to doorstep — and the build system behind it"}]});
COURSE_LESSONS["uber-api-layer"]={"id":"uber-api-layer","title":"How Uber’s API layer evolved","summary":"Uber’s front door changed three times — from two services to a 2,200-service estate behind one gateway, then to self-service layered edge ownership — while the nearby-driver problem is solved with geohash cells rather than distance maths over every row.","minutes":17,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"Three generations of the API layer","step":0},{"text":"The dispatch / API split","step":1},{"text":"The all-encompassing gateway","step":4},{"text":"Self-service, decentralised, layered","step":6},{"text":"Business and location services","step":8},{"text":"Geohash and prefix search","step":10},{"text":"Skew and the trade-offs","step":13}],"steps":[{"short":"Three acts","chapter":"setup","title":"The front door moved three times","body":["The guide on Uber’s API layer tells the story in three generations: an organic start, one all-encompassing gateway, and a self-service design where ownership moves out to the teams. The product kept working while the shape of its front door changed underneath it.","That is the useful lesson. An API layer is not a fixed box in the diagram; it is a boundary that gets redrawn as the company and its product lines grow. A second guide adds the other half of a ride request: finding what is nearby.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"An API gateway is an organisational decision as much as a technical one."},"changes":["+ Three generations","+ Two guides, two halves of a ride"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"g1","type":"block","x":-11,"y":0,"label":"gen 1: two services","color":"blue"},{"k":"g2","type":"block","x":0,"y":0,"label":"gen 2: one gateway","color":"orange"},{"k":"g3","type":"block","x":11,"y":0,"label":"gen 3: layered","color":"violet"}],"links":[{"from":"g1","to":"g2"},{"from":"g2","to":"g3"}]}},{"short":"First generation","chapter":"technique","title":"Two services, one product","body":["In the 2014 picture the product rested on <b>two key services</b>. The <b>dispatch service</b> connected a rider with a driver; the <b>API service</b> held the long-term data about users and trips.","This is what the guide calls organic evolution: responsibilities were carved along the two jobs the company actually had — a live matching job and a durable record job — rather than along an abstract domain model.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Split along the workload you have, not the organisation you plan to have."},"changes":["+ Dispatch: live matching","+ API: users and trips"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"r","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"rider","color":"ink"},{"k":"api","type":"block","x":-4,"y":-5,"label":"API service","color":"blue","sub":"users · trips"},{"k":"d","type":"block","x":-4,"y":6,"label":"dispatch","color":"violet","sub":"rider ↔ driver"},{"k":"store","type":"db","x":7,"y":-5,"label":"long-term data","color":"green"},{"k":"drivers","type":"crowd","x0":12,"x1":16,"y0":2,"y1":10,"label":"drivers","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"r","to":"api"},{"from":"r","to":"d"},{"from":"api","to":"store","label":"records"},{"from":"d","to":"drivers","label":"match"}]}},{"short":"Two jobs","chapter":"insight","title":"Why this split was enough at first","body":["Matching is ephemeral and time-critical; account and trip records are durable and queried for years. Separating them let each service change on its own schedule, which is exactly what a small, fast-moving team needs.","There is nothing naive about this stage. The test of a boundary comes later, when the number of products and the rate of change both multiply.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Early boundaries are usually about workload shape, not org charts."},"changes":["+ Fast path for matching","+ Slow path for records"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Two jobs","lines":["dispatch → live match, short-lived","API      → users and trips, durable","boundary → different change rates"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"New lines","chapter":"problem","title":"When two services were not enough","body":["The guide introduces the third generation through new business lines: freight, autonomous driving, elevation projects and groceries, among others. Each arrives with its own clients, data and release cadence.","A front door built for one ride-hailing product is now asked to serve several products that barely share a domain. That pressure is what pushes a gateway to change shape.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A gateway designed for one product becomes a queue for every new product."},"changes":["+ Freight · ATG · Elevate · groceries","− One door for every line"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"api","type":"block","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"API service","color":"orange"},{"k":"f","type":"block","x":-2,"y":-8,"label":"Freight","color":"blue"},{"k":"a","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"ATG","color":"blue"},{"k":"g","type":"block","x":-2,"y":8,"label":"Groceries","color":"blue"},{"k":"apps","type":"block","x":11,"y":0,"label":"new apps","color":"violet"}],"links":[{"from":"api","to":"f"},{"from":"api","to":"a"},{"from":"api","to":"g"},{"from":"f","to":"apps"},{"from":"a","to":"apps"},{"from":"g","to":"apps"}]}},{"short":"One gateway","chapter":"technique","title":"The all-encompassing gateway","body":["In the second generation Uber leaned into microservices early. By 2019 the guide counts <b>more than 2,200 microservices</b> powering its products, with one all-encompassing gateway in front of them.","Clients now talk to a single edge. Authentication, routing and other rules that must hold for every request can live in one place instead of being repeated by every service.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"A single entry point is where the rules that must always hold belong."},"changes":["+ All-encompassing API gateway","+ 2,200+ microservices"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cl","type":"crowd","x0":-16,"x1":-11,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"clients","color":"ink"},{"k":"gw","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"API gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"s1","type":"block","x":7,"y":-7,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"s2","type":"block","x":7,"y":0,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"s3","type":"block","x":7,"y":7,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"data","type":"db","x":15,"y":0,"label":"data","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"cl","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"s1"},{"from":"gw","to":"s2"},{"from":"gw","to":"s3"},{"from":"s2","to":"data"}]}},{"short":"One door","chapter":"problem","title":"The cost of a single front door","body":["The concentration that makes shared rules easy also makes the gateway a meeting point for every team. A change to shared gateway code can hold up unrelated products, and all traffic and latency pile up at one edge.","This is a reading of the shape the guide describes, not a claim that the gateway failed. The guide says the third generation arrived because a new set of goals called for a different boundary.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A shared boundary buys consistency at the cost of local freedom."},"changes":["+ Shared rules in one place","− Independent releases"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"t1","type":"block","x":-14,"y":-7,"label":"team A","color":"blue"},{"k":"t2","type":"block","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"team B","color":"blue"},{"k":"t3","type":"block","x":-14,"y":7,"label":"team C","color":"blue"},{"k":"gw","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"one gateway","color":"orange","sub":"all traffic"},{"k":"pool","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"services","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"t1","to":"gw","label":"changes"},{"from":"t2","to":"gw"},{"from":"t3","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"pool"}]}},{"short":"Third generation","chapter":"technique","title":"Self-service, decentralised, layered","body":["The third generation is described with three words: <b>self-service, decentralised and layered</b>. Teams own more of the path to their own services instead of sending every change through one gatekeeping layer.","Decentralised does not mean unstructured. ‘Layered’ is the other half: responsibilities are still ordered, but the work is arranged so teams can move within them.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Move the boundary; keep the layering."},"changes":["+ Self-service edge","+ Decentralised ownership","+ Layered responsibilities"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"z","type":"zone","x0":-16,"x1":16,"y0":-10,"y1":10,"label":"layered edge"},{"k":"ride","type":"block","x":-10,"y":-2,"label":"Rides gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"fr","type":"block","x":0,"y":-2,"label":"Freight gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"gr","type":"block","x":10,"y":-2,"label":"Grocery gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"rs","type":"db","x":-10,"y":7,"label":"rides services","color":"blue"},{"k":"fs","type":"db","x":0,"y":7,"label":"freight services","color":"blue"},{"k":"gs","type":"db","x":10,"y":7,"label":"grocery services","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"ride","to":"rs"},{"from":"fr","to":"fs"},{"from":"gr","to":"gs"}]}},{"short":"Self-service","chapter":"insight","title":"Ownership is a scaling tool","body":["When a team can ship its own edge rules, it no longer waits in a central queue. The platform’s job shifts from writing every rule to providing a safe, repeatable way for teams to write their own.","That is the general pattern behind the third generation: keep the invariants in shared tooling and move the decisions to the people closest to the product.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Standardise the mechanism, not every decision."},"changes":["+ Team-owned edge rules","+ Platform-provided guardrails"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The split","lines":["platform → safe defaults and tooling","teams    → product-specific rules","result   → independent releases"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Nearby","chapter":"technique","title":"The other half of a ride: proximity","body":["A ride request needs more than a front door; it needs to know which drivers are close. The proximity guide poses the same problem with restaurants: how do you find nearby places on a map quickly?","It names two services. A <b>business service</b> adds, deletes and updates restaurant information and serves detail views. A <b>location-based service</b> takes a radius and a location and returns the nearby set.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A writable catalogue and a read-heavy lookup deserve separate services."},"changes":["+ Business service: catalogue","+ Location service: nearby search"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"q","type":"device","x":-14,"y":-4,"label":"nearby query","color":"ink"},{"k":"lbs","type":"block","x":-4,"y":-4,"label":"location service","color":"violet","sub":"radius + point"},{"k":"near","type":"db","x":7,"y":-4,"label":"nearby list","color":"green"},{"k":"biz","type":"block","x":-4,"y":7,"label":"business service","color":"blue"},{"k":"cat","type":"db","x":7,"y":7,"label":"restaurant data","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"q","to":"lbs"},{"from":"lbs","to":"near"},{"from":"biz","to":"cat"},{"from":"cat","to":"lbs","label":"locations"}]}},{"short":"Naive query","chapter":"problem","title":"Why lat/lon columns are not enough","body":["The obvious design stores each place’s latitude and longitude and computes the distance to every row at query time. The guide rejects that as inefficient: every search would make the database scan the whole catalogue.","The problem is not the arithmetic; it is the number of comparisons. A radius search has to shrink the candidate set before doing precise work.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A correct distance function over every row is still the wrong query plan."},"changes":["− Full-table distance scan"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"all","type":"crowd","x0":-16,"x1":-10,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"all rows","color":"ink"},{"k":"dist","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"distance to every row","color":"orange"},{"k":"out","type":"db","x":12,"y":0,"label":"matches","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"all","to":"dist","label":"scan all"},{"from":"dist","to":"out"}]}},{"short":"Geohash","chapter":"technique","title":"Turn coordinates into a sortable cell","body":["The guide’s answer is <b>geohash</b>. Split the planet into four quadrants along the prime meridian and the equator: a southern latitude is bit 0, a northern one bit 1; a western longitude is 0, an eastern one 1.","Then subdivide each cell into four again, alternating longitude and latitude bits. A location becomes a short string whose prefix names a cell, and cells nest — a longer prefix means a smaller area.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Encode location as a prefix, then let a normal index do the work."},"changes":["+ Geohash encoding","+ Nested grid cells"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"ll","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"latitude / longitude","color":"ink"},{"k":"bits","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"alternating bits","color":"violet"},{"k":"cell","type":"db","x":5,"y":0,"label":"cell string","color":"green"},{"k":"idx","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"geohash index","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"ll","to":"bits"},{"from":"bits","to":"cell"},{"from":"cell","to":"idx"}]}},{"short":"Prefix search","chapter":"technique","title":"The search becomes a prefix lookup","body":["With geohashes indexed, a cell query is a prefix lookup — the guide shows SQL of the form <code>SELECT * FROM geohash_index WHERE geohash LIKE ‘01%’</code>. An index range scan replaces a full-table distance calculation.","The catch is discreteness: a circle around the user rarely lines up with a grid rectangle. A real near-search also checks neighbouring cells, then applies an exact distance test to the small survivor set.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Use the grid to shrink the set; use real distance only on the finalists."},"changes":["+ Prefix lookup via index","+ Adjacent cells","+ Exact distance on finalists"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cell","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"target cell","color":"ink"},{"k":"idx","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"geohash index","color":"blue"},{"k":"cands","type":"crowd","x0":4,"x1":8,"y0":-7,"y1":7,"label":"candidate cells","color":"violet"},{"k":"dist","type":"block","x":13,"y":0,"label":"distance check","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"cell","to":"idx"},{"from":"idx","to":"cands"},{"from":"cands","to":"dist","label":"finalists"}]}},{"short":"Skew","chapter":"problem","title":"A grid that is never evenly full","body":["The guide is candid about geohash’s main weakness: cells are a fixed size, but people are not. Downtown Manhattan can hold a large number of restaurants in one cell while a cell over the ocean holds none.","Longer prefixes make cells smaller but cannot fix the imbalance. The guide notes that more elaborate algorithms exist to optimise the process; the goal is to keep cell occupancy practical.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"A uniform grid over a non-uniform world makes hotspots and empty cells."},"changes":["+ Uneven cell occupancy","− Uniform-density assumption"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"city","type":"crowd","x0":-15,"x1":-8,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"city cell: many","color":"ink"},{"k":"grid","type":"block","x":-1,"y":0,"label":"fixed grid","color":"orange"},{"k":"sea","type":"crowd","x0":7,"x1":14,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"ocean cell: none","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"city","to":"grid"},{"from":"grid","to":"sea"}]}},{"short":"Trade-offs","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"Picking a location index","body":["Geohash buys a simple, index-friendly query at the price of boundary handling and skew. Adaptive or hierarchical schemes attack the imbalance with extra machinery, which is why the guide calls them more complicated rather than a drop-in fix.","The same calculus applies to Uber’s gateways: the simple design is right until scale or organisation makes its assumption false. Both stories are about where to put a boundary so the cheap work happens first.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Both halves reward the same habit: let the cheap thing do the coarse work."},"changes":["+ Coarse index, precise finish","+ Boundaries chosen for the workload"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The balance","lines":["geohash → cheap prefix queries","skew    → dense and empty cells","fix     → finer or adaptive cells","gateway → move the boundary with the org"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"Two maps of the same city","body":["Uber’s API layer went from dispatch plus API, to one gateway in front of 2,200+ microservices, to a self-service, decentralised and layered edge. The proximity guide supplies the other side: a catalogue service and a location service, with geohash turning radius search into prefix lookups.","One story is about where requests enter; the other is about finding what is near. Both are lessons in putting the boundary where the workload makes it cheap.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/evolution-of-uber's-api-layer.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Evolution of Uber’s API layer</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/proximity-service.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proximity service</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"Next: <b>Airbnb’s architectural evolution</b> — a Rails monolith broken into four service roles."},"changes":["+ Three API generations","+ Geohash prefix lookup","+ Cheap coarse pass before precision"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The rule","lines":["edge   → move the boundary as teams grow","nearby → prefix first, distance after","skew   → expect uneven cells"]}],"links":[]}}]};
COURSE_LESSONS["airbnb-soa"]={"id":"airbnb-soa","title":"From Monorail to services at Airbnb","summary":"Airbnb grew from a single Ruby-on-Rails ‘Monorail’ into a gateway-fronted service architecture with four distinct roles — data, derived data, middle tier and presentation — and a full cutover of reads and writes.","minutes":14,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"The Rails Monorail","step":1},{"text":"Why hypergrowth forced a split","step":3},{"text":"The gateway front door","step":4},{"text":"Data and derived-data services","step":5},{"text":"Middle-tier and presentation services","step":7},{"text":"Allowed dependency directions","step":9},{"text":"The final cutover and its costs","step":10}],"steps":[{"short":"Planetary scale","chapter":"setup","title":"A booking site at planetary scale","body":["The guide’s headline numbers are hard to ignore: Airbnb operates in more than <b>200 countries</b>, and around <b>4 million hosts</b> have welcomed over <b>1.5 billion guests</b>. A codebase that served the first users will not automatically serve that.","What follows is a migration story: how a monolith was replaced by a service-oriented architecture, one service role at a time.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Scale changes the right answer, not the original answer."},"changes":["+ 200+ countries","+ 1.5B guests"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"g","type":"crowd","x0":-16,"x1":-11,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"guests","color":"ink"},{"k":"ab","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"Airbnb","color":"violet","sub":"platform"},{"k":"h","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"hosts","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"g","to":"ab","label":"book"},{"from":"ab","to":"h","label":"stay"}]}},{"short":"Monorail","chapter":"technique","title":"One app, both tiers","body":["Airbnb started as a <b>Ruby on Rails monolith</b> known internally as the <b>Monorail</b>. The guide describes it as a single-tier unit responsible for both client-side and server-side functionality: one application produced the interface and also owned the data path.","That is a reasonable beginning. Everything lives in one place, so there is one build, one deploy and one system to inspect when something breaks.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Monoliths buy early speed by keeping a change in one repository and one process."},"changes":["+ Ruby on Rails monolith","+ One tier: client and server"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"z","type":"zone","x0":-17,"x1":17,"y0":-10,"y1":10,"label":"Monorail"},{"k":"web","type":"device","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"web client","color":"ink"},{"k":"mono","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"Monorail","color":"orange","sub":"Rails"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":11,"y":0,"label":"database","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"web","to":"mono"},{"from":"mono","to":"db"}]}},{"short":"Early win","chapter":"insight","title":"The monolith was not a mistake","body":["In a single-tier application the common case is cheap: a feature touches one codebase and ships in one release. The guide tells this chronologically rather than critically — the Monorail served Airbnb through its early years.","The decision to leave arrives when growth makes the cost of one big unit larger than the cost of running many small ones.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Architecture is judged against the scale it had to serve at the time."},"changes":["+ One codebase, one release"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"Early win","lines":["change → one repository","deploy → one artefact","debug  → one call path"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Hypergrowth","chapter":"problem","title":"When the single tier gets crowded","body":["As Airbnb entered hypergrowth the Monorail began to face issues, and the team began moving toward <b>service-oriented architecture</b>. The guide does not linger on each symptom; the direction is the point.","In an SOA a client request reaches a <b>gateway</b>, which routes it to multiple services and databases. The monolith’s in-process calls become network calls, with all the extra failure modes that implies.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Splitting a monolith turns in-process calls into network dependencies."},"changes":["− Monorail","+ Gateway plus loosely coupled services"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cl","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"client","color":"ink"},{"k":"gw","type":"block","x":-5,"y":0,"label":"gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"s1","type":"block","x":5,"y":-7,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"s2","type":"block","x":5,"y":0,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"s3","type":"block","x":5,"y":7,"label":"service","color":"blue"},{"k":"d","type":"db","x":15,"y":0,"label":"databases","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"cl","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"s1"},{"from":"gw","to":"s2"},{"from":"gw","to":"s3"},{"from":"s2","to":"d"}]}},{"short":"Gateway","chapter":"technique","title":"One front door, many routes","body":["The gateway is the front door. Clients do not need to know which service owns which field; the gateway resolves a request into several service calls and returns a composed answer.","That makes it the natural spot for cross-cutting work, but the guide’s emphasis is routing into a network of loosely coupled services, not a clever layer that takes over business logic.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Keep the gateway about routing and composition; let services own the rules."},"changes":["+ Gateway routes client requests","+ Services own their data"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cl","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"client","color":"ink"},{"k":"gw","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"p","type":"block","x":6,"y":-7,"label":"service A","color":"blue"},{"k":"q","type":"block","x":6,"y":7,"label":"service B","color":"blue"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":15,"y":0,"label":"service data","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"cl","to":"gw"},{"from":"gw","to":"p"},{"from":"gw","to":"q"},{"from":"p","to":"db"},{"from":"q","to":"db"}]}},{"short":"Data service","chapter":"technique","title":"The bottom layer owns the entity","body":["The first of four service types is the <b>data service</b>. It sits at the bottom layer and acts as the entry point for every read and write of a given data entity.","One owner per entity is the important part: other services go through this layer instead of reaching into the same tables. That is what makes a later schema change a local decision.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Ownership is a service boundary, not a naming convention."},"changes":["+ Data service: sole entity entry point"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"up","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"caller services","color":"violet"},{"k":"ds","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"data service","color":"green","sub":"sole entry"},{"k":"db","type":"db","x":11,"y":0,"label":"entity tables","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"up","to":"ds","label":"all reads / writes"},{"from":"ds","to":"db"}]}},{"short":"Derived data","chapter":"technique","title":"Reads with a little logic","body":["<b>Derived data services</b> read from data services and apply basic business logic. They do not introduce a second source of truth; they reshape or summarise what the data layer already holds.","Keeping derived results one hop from the authoritative service avoids the familiar problem of copies drifting away from the original.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Build derived views from the owning service, not from a second copy you must also maintain."},"changes":["+ Derived data services","+ Basic logic over owned entities"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"ds","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"data service","color":"green"},{"k":"dd","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"derived data","color":"violet"},{"k":"view","type":"db","x":9,"y":0,"label":"view / summary","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"ds","to":"dd","label":"read"},{"from":"dd","to":"view"}]}},{"short":"Middle tier","chapter":"technique","title":"Logic that belongs nowhere lower","body":["Some rules do not fit at the data layer or in a derived view. The guide places them in <b>middle-tier services</b>, which manage the important business logic that does not belong at the data or derived-data level.","This is where workflows spanning several entities live — rules that no single data service should be told about.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Put cross-entity rules above entities, not inside one of them."},"changes":["+ Middle-tier services for cross-entity logic"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"d1","type":"db","x":-13,"y":-7,"label":"data service A","color":"green"},{"k":"d2","type":"db","x":-13,"y":7,"label":"data service B","color":"green"},{"k":"mid","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"middle tier","color":"orange","sub":"cross-entity rules"},{"k":"out","type":"db","x":11,"y":0,"label":"composed result","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"d1","to":"mid"},{"from":"d2","to":"mid"},{"from":"mid","to":"out"}]}},{"short":"Presentation","chapter":"technique","title":"The last hop before the client","body":["<b>Presentation services</b> aggregate data from the other services and apply frontend-specific business logic. They are the composition layer that a gateway-routed request eventually reaches.","Keeping device-specific shaping up here lets the lower layers stay about data and rules while the response format changes with the client.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Let the top layer speak the client’s language and the lower layers speak the domain’s."},"changes":["+ Presentation services aggregate","+ Frontend logic at the edge"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"d","type":"db","x":-14,"y":-6,"label":"data services","color":"green"},{"k":"dd","type":"block","x":-6,"y":-6,"label":"derived","color":"violet"},{"k":"mid","type":"block","x":2,"y":0,"label":"middle tier","color":"orange"},{"k":"pres","type":"block","x":10,"y":0,"label":"presentation","color":"blue"},{"k":"cl","type":"device","x":15,"y":-8,"label":"client","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"d","to":"dd"},{"from":"dd","to":"mid"},{"from":"mid","to":"pres"},{"from":"pres","to":"cl"}]}},{"short":"Allowed directions","chapter":"insight","title":"The layering is the architecture","body":["Read the roles as an ordering: data services own entities, derived services reshape them, middle-tier services handle logic across them, and presentation services compose the answer. The gateway starts the request at the top.","The value lies in knowing which way calls may travel. Once every layer can call every other layer, the service graph becomes a distributed monolith with worse latency.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Loosely coupled only helps when the dependencies stay intentional."},"changes":["+ Ordered dependencies","− Any-to-any calls"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"gw","type":"block","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"gateway","color":"violet"},{"k":"pr","type":"block","x":-7,"y":0,"label":"presentation","color":"blue"},{"k":"mid","type":"block","x":0,"y":0,"label":"middle tier","color":"orange"},{"k":"dd","type":"block","x":7,"y":0,"label":"derived data","color":"violet"},{"k":"ds","type":"block","x":14,"y":0,"label":"data service","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"gw","to":"pr"},{"from":"pr","to":"mid"},{"from":"mid","to":"dd"},{"from":"dd","to":"ds"}]}},{"short":"Cutover","chapter":"problem","title":"Moving every read and write","body":["The guide is explicit about the end state: after the migration the Monorail was eliminated and <b>all reads and writes were migrated to the new services</b>. A partial migration leaves two owners for the same data, which is the worst of both designs.","That is the hard part of a monolith-to-SOA move — not standing up new services, but draining the old path of its last caller.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Two write paths for one entity means two sources of truth."},"changes":["− Monorail reads / writes","+ One owner per entity"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"mono","type":"block","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"Monorail","color":"orange"},{"k":"cut","type":"block","x":-2,"y":0,"label":"migrate all traffic","color":"violet"},{"k":"svc","type":"crowd","x0":10,"x1":16,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"new services","color":"ink"},{"k":"dup","type":"block","x":-2,"y":8,"label":"partial = two owners","color":"red"}],"links":[{"from":"mono","to":"cut","label":"cutover"},{"from":"cut","to":"svc"},{"from":"mono","to":"dup","label":"avoid"}]}},{"short":"Autonomy cost","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"More boxes, more operational work","body":["Four service roles plus a gateway mean more deployments, more network hops and more places a request can fail. The payoff in the guide is a system able to keep growing past the monolith’s comfortable range.","It is the standard trade: accept operational complexity in exchange for independent change and clearer ownership.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Microservices scale organisations as much as they scale traffic."},"changes":["+ Independent deploys","− Simple single-process debugging"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The balance","lines":["monolith → simple to run, hard to grow","SOA      → many owners, more failure points","rule     → split where change rates differ"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"Four roles, one front door","body":["The guide moves from the Rails Monorail to a gateway in front of loosely coupled services: data services own entities, derived data services apply basic logic, middle-tier services hold cross-entity rules and presentation services compose responses.","The Monorail was removed only after all traffic had moved. That last step is the one most worth remembering.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/airbnb-artchitectural-evolution.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">0 to 1.5 Billion Guests: Airbnb’s Architectural Evolution</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"Next: <b>the e-commerce workflow</b> — four business areas, from supplier to doorstep."},"changes":["+ Gateway","+ Four service roles","+ Full cutover from the monolith"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The rule","lines":["own        → data services","reshape    → derived data","coordinate → middle tier","compose    → presentation"]}],"links":[]}}]};
COURSE_LESSONS["ecommerce-workflow"]={"id":"ecommerce-workflow","title":"From cart to doorstep — and the build system behind it","summary":"An online order crosses procurement, inventory, the e-commerce platform and transportation, while Amazon’s Brazil build system keeps the software behind it reproducible through version sets and DAG builds.","minutes":16,"level":"intermediate","goals":[{"text":"The four business areas","step":0},{"text":"From supplier to listing","step":1},{"text":"Browse, cart, reserve, pay","step":4},{"text":"Delivery and order splitting","step":6},{"text":"Brazil’s build configuration and DAG","step":9},{"text":"Version sets and the package builder","step":11},{"text":"Reproducibility and trade-offs","step":13}],"steps":[{"short":"Four areas","chapter":"setup","title":"A purchase crosses four businesses","body":["The e-commerce guide organises a retailer into <b>four business areas</b>: procurement, inventory, the e-commerce platform and transportation. A single purchase touches all four, even though a shopper only sees the last two.","Its author is upfront that the diagram is research-based rather than an insider account of one company — a useful reminder to read it as a map of responsibilities.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A checkout is the visible tip of a supply chain."},"changes":["+ Procurement","+ Inventory","+ E-commerce platform","+ Transportation"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"block","x":-12,"y":0,"label":"procurement","color":"orange"},{"k":"i","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"inventory","color":"blue"},{"k":"e","type":"block","x":4,"y":0,"label":"eComm platform","color":"violet"},{"k":"t","type":"block","x":12,"y":0,"label":"transportation","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"p","to":"i"},{"from":"i","to":"e"},{"from":"e","to":"t"}]}},{"short":"Procurement","chapter":"technique","title":"Choosing suppliers and placing orders","body":["Procurement selects suppliers and manages the contracts with them. It also places orders, handles the return of goods and settles invoices.","Notice that this is a commercial relationship, not a technical one: the first two steps in the guide’s numbered workflow happen long before any shopping app is involved.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"The catalogue starts with contracts, not with database rows."},"changes":["+ Supplier selection and contracts","+ Orders, returns, invoices"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"block","x":-8,"y":0,"label":"procurement","color":"orange"},{"k":"s","type":"crowd","x0":7,"x1":14,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"suppliers","color":"ink"},{"k":"c","type":"db","x":-8,"y":-9,"label":"contracts","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"p","to":"s","label":"orders"},{"from":"s","to":"p","label":"returns","heads":"both"},{"from":"c","to":"p"}]}},{"short":"Inventory","chapter":"technique","title":"Goods arrive and get counted","body":["Products from suppliers are delivered to a storage facility, where an <b>inventory management system</b> keeps track of them. This is the bridge between the physical and digital halves of the business.","Every later promise to a customer — ‘in stock’, ‘ships today’ — is a claim about this system’s state.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A stock count is a promise to a future shopper."},"changes":["+ Storage facilities","+ Inventory management system"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"s","type":"crowd","x0":-16,"x1":-11,"y0":-8,"y1":8,"label":"suppliers","color":"ink"},{"k":"w","type":"block","x":-3,"y":0,"label":"storage facility","color":"blue"},{"k":"im","type":"db","x":10,"y":0,"label":"inventory system","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"s","to":"w","label":"goods"},{"from":"w","to":"im","label":"counts"}]}},{"short":"Product data","chapter":"technique","title":"Turning goods into listings","body":["Inside the e-commerce platform, product management creates the product information and the pricing system prices the products; then they are ready to be listed for sale. The promotion system defines big sales, coupons and similar activities.","These are separate concerns because they change for different reasons: a description, a price and a discount have different owners and lifetimes.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Keep product facts, prices and promotions apart; each changes on its own clock."},"changes":["+ Product information","+ Pricing","+ Promotions"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"g","type":"db","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"goods","color":"ink"},{"k":"pm","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"product mgmt","color":"violet"},{"k":"pr","type":"block","x":2,"y":0,"label":"pricing","color":"blue"},{"k":"list","type":"db","x":12,"y":-5,"label":"listing","color":"green"},{"k":"promo","type":"block","x":2,"y":8,"label":"promotions","color":"orange"}],"links":[{"from":"g","to":"pm"},{"from":"pm","to":"pr"},{"from":"pr","to":"list"},{"from":"promo","to":"list","label":"discounts"}]}},{"short":"Customer path","chapter":"technique","title":"Browse, cart, order","body":["Shoppers register or log in, browse the product list and details, add products to a shopping cart and then place a purchasing order. The guide numbers these as steps eight through eleven.","Everything before the order is read-heavy and cheap to cache; the order is the moment the system must become certain about stock and money.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Browsing is a read problem; checkout is a consistency problem."},"changes":["+ Register / login","+ Browse and cart","+ Place order"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"u","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"shopper","color":"ink"},{"k":"app","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"eComm app","color":"violet"},{"k":"cat","type":"db","x":2,"y":-7,"label":"catalogue","color":"blue","sub":"browse"},{"k":"cart","type":"db","x":2,"y":7,"label":"cart","color":"green"},{"k":"ord","type":"db","x":12,"y":0,"label":"order","color":"orange"}],"links":[{"from":"u","to":"app"},{"from":"app","to":"cat"},{"from":"app","to":"cart"},{"from":"cart","to":"ord","label":"checkout"}]}},{"short":"Reserve and pay","chapter":"technique","title":"Two commitments at checkout","body":["The order management system reserves stock in the inventory management system, and then the user pays for the product. The guide puts those steps in that order deliberately: reservation comes first.","Reserving before charging avoids taking money for a basket the warehouse cannot fulfil. Holding a reservation for too long creates the opposite problem, so these systems need expiry rules.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"warn","text":"Charge before you reserve and you may have to refund a promise you cannot keep."},"changes":["+ Reserve stock","+ Then take payment","+ Reservation expiry"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"o","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"order","color":"ink"},{"k":"oms","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"order mgmt","color":"violet"},{"k":"inv","type":"db","x":5,"y":0,"label":"reserve stock","color":"green"},{"k":"pay","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"payment","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"o","to":"oms"},{"from":"oms","to":"inv","label":"reserve"},{"from":"inv","to":"pay","label":"then charge"}]}},{"short":"Fulfilment","chapter":"technique","title":"The outbound order leaves the building","body":["Once stock is committed, the inventory system sends the outbound order to the transportation system, which manages the physical delivery of the goods. The guide ends with an optional step: signing for the delivery.","The hand-off is worth noticing. The digital system’s job ends when the parcel is with the carrier; the physical world takes over until tracking and delivery bring the two back together.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Model the hand-off explicitly — tracking is where the digital and physical stories rejoin."},"changes":["+ Outbound order","+ Transportation","+ Optional signature"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"inv","type":"db","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"inventory","color":"green"},{"k":"out","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"outbound order","color":"violet"},{"k":"tr","type":"block","x":5,"y":0,"label":"transportation","color":"orange"},{"k":"cust","type":"device","x":14,"y":0,"label":"delivery / sign","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"inv","to":"out"},{"from":"out","to":"tr"},{"from":"tr","to":"cust","label":"deliver"}]}},{"short":"Order splitting","chapter":"problem","title":"One basket, several warehouses","body":["The guide closes with a question rather than an answer: a large basket may be divided into several smaller orders based on warehouse location, product type and so on. Where should the <b>order splitting</b> system sit?","A natural place is between order management and inventory reservation, because each sub-order is fulfilled from a different place and stock must be promised from the right one. That placement is a design judgement offered here, not a claim from the guide.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Split before you reserve, or you will reserve from the wrong warehouse."},"changes":["+ One order → several fulfilments","+ Split before reservation"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"big","type":"db","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"big order","color":"ink"},{"k":"split","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"order splitting","color":"orange","sub":"a proposed home"},{"k":"s1","type":"db","x":5,"y":-8,"label":"sub-order","color":"green"},{"k":"s2","type":"db","x":5,"y":0,"label":"sub-order","color":"green"},{"k":"s3","type":"db","x":5,"y":8,"label":"sub-order","color":"green"},{"k":"wh","type":"db","x":15,"y":0,"label":"warehouses","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"big","to":"split"},{"from":"split","to":"s1"},{"from":"split","to":"s2"},{"from":"split","to":"s3"},{"from":"s2","to":"wh"}]}},{"short":"Two flows","chapter":"insight","title":"Information moves one way, goods the other","body":["Read the four areas as two loops. Procurement and inventory move goods toward the shelf; product, pricing, promotion, cart and payment move information toward an order; transportation turns that order into a physical delivery.","The interfaces between areas are where the interesting systems live: stock reservations, outbound orders and delivery status.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"The seams between business areas are the systems worth designing carefully."},"changes":["+ Goods flow in","+ Information flow out","+ Delivery closes the loop"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"z","type":"zone","x0":-16,"x1":16,"y0":-11,"y1":11,"label":"supply chain"},{"k":"p","type":"block","x":-11,"y":-6,"label":"procurement","color":"orange"},{"k":"i","type":"block","x":-2,"y":-6,"label":"inventory","color":"blue"},{"k":"e","type":"block","x":-2,"y":6,"label":"eComm platform","color":"violet"},{"k":"t","type":"block","x":8,"y":0,"label":"transportation","color":"green"},{"k":"u","type":"device","x":15,"y":7,"label":"customer","color":"ink"}],"links":[{"from":"p","to":"i","label":"goods"},{"from":"i","to":"e","label":"stock"},{"from":"e","to":"t","label":"outbound"},{"from":"t","to":"u","label":"parcel","heads":"both"}]}},{"short":"Brazil","chapter":"technique","title":"Amazon’s build system","body":["The second guide switches from goods to code. Amazon’s ownership model gives each team its own repositories, and <b>Brazil</b> is the build system created to make that practical: developers write code and a small build configuration instead of wrestling with the build itself.","That configuration declares what the build needs — language, versioning, dependencies, major versions and how version conflicts are resolved.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"A declared build config is a contract between a team and the build system."},"changes":["+ Micro-repo ownership","+ Declarative build config"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"team","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"team repo","color":"ink"},{"k":"cfg","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"build config","color":"violet"},{"k":"bz","type":"block","x":5,"y":0,"label":"Brazil","color":"orange"},{"k":"art","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"artifact","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"team","to":"cfg"},{"from":"cfg","to":"bz"},{"from":"bz","to":"art"}]}},{"short":"DAG","chapter":"technique","title":"Local builds as a graph","body":["For a local build, Brazil interprets the configuration as a <b>directed acyclic graph</b>. It fetches packages from a private space called a <b>version set</b> — the guide gives <code>myservice-cpp-version-set</code> as an example — generates the language-specific build configuration and invokes the appropriate build tool.","The graph is what makes the steps repeatable: with the same inputs and the same tool, the same artifact should come out.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Describe a build as a graph and let the tool decide the execution order."},"changes":["+ Config → DAG","+ Version-set retrieval","+ Language-specific build files"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"cfg","type":"db","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"build config","color":"ink"},{"k":"dag","type":"block","x":-6,"y":0,"label":"DAG","color":"violet"},{"k":"vs","type":"db","x":2,"y":0,"label":"version set","color":"blue"},{"k":"tool","type":"block","x":10,"y":0,"label":"build tool","color":"orange"},{"k":"art","type":"db","x":10,"y":9,"label":"artifact","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"cfg","to":"dag"},{"from":"dag","to":"vs","label":"packages"},{"from":"vs","to":"tool"},{"from":"tool","to":"art"}]}},{"short":"Version sets","chapter":"technique","title":"Private space, public default","body":["A <b>version set</b> is a collection of package versions that gives a package and its dependencies a private space. When a new dependency is introduced, it must also be merged into that private space.","There is also a default set called <b>live</b>, a public space where anyone can publish any version. The pair gives teams isolation by default and a deliberate route to sharing.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Isolation is a namespace you choose, not a hope that nobody breaks you."},"changes":["+ Private version sets","+ Public ‘live’ set","+ Dependencies merged explicitly"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"z","type":"zone","x0":-18,"x1":18,"y0":-10,"y1":10,"label":"version space"},{"k":"team","type":"block","x":-13,"y":0,"label":"team","color":"ink"},{"k":"priv","type":"db","x":-4,"y":-4,"label":"private version set","color":"violet"},{"k":"live","type":"db","x":7,"y":-4,"label":"live (public)","color":"blue"},{"k":"rel","type":"block","x":-4,"y":7,"label":"new dependency","color":"orange"},{"k":"pub","type":"block","x":7,"y":7,"label":"publish","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"team","to":"priv"},{"from":"rel","to":"priv","label":"merge"},{"from":"priv","to":"live","label":"promote"},{"from":"pub","to":"live"}]}},{"short":"Package builder","chapter":"technique","title":"Builds on demand, remotely","body":["Remotely, a <b>package builder service</b> lets an engineer pick a version set and the targets to build. The guide says it supports Amazon Linux on x86, x64 and ARM.","A build can be started manually or automatically on a new commit to the master branch. The same configuration drives both paths.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"tip","text":"Manual and CI builds should call the same service with the same inputs."},"changes":["+ Package builder service","+ x86 · x64 · ARM","+ Manual or commit-triggered"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"eng","type":"device","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"engineer","color":"ink"},{"k":"cm","type":"db","x":-14,"y":-9,"label":"master commit","color":"green"},{"k":"pb","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"package builder","color":"violet"},{"k":"x86","type":"db","x":7,"y":-8,"label":"x86","color":"blue"},{"k":"x64","type":"db","x":7,"y":0,"label":"x64","color":"blue"},{"k":"arm","type":"db","x":7,"y":8,"label":"ARM","color":"blue"}],"links":[{"from":"eng","to":"pb"},{"from":"cm","to":"pb","label":"auto"},{"from":"pb","to":"x86"},{"from":"pb","to":"x64"},{"from":"pb","to":"arm"}]}},{"short":"Reproducibility","chapter":"technique","title":"Snapshots and versioned outputs","body":["The package builder’s promise is consistency and reproducibility: each build process is snapshotted and the output artifact is versioned. A build is not a one-off action; it is a recorded event that can be re-run.","That is what makes a micro-repo organisation viable. Without it, every team’s private version set would be a private mystery.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Reproducibility is what turns autonomous builds into trustworthy ones."},"changes":["+ Snapshot each build","+ Version each artifact"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"trig","type":"db","x":-14,"y":0,"label":"commit + config","color":"ink"},{"k":"b","type":"block","x":-4,"y":0,"label":"build","color":"violet"},{"k":"snap","type":"db","x":6,"y":0,"label":"snapshot","color":"blue"},{"k":"art","type":"db","x":14,"y":0,"label":"versioned artifact","color":"green"}],"links":[{"from":"trig","to":"b"},{"from":"b","to":"snap"},{"from":"snap","to":"art"}]}},{"short":"One idea","chapter":"tradeoff","title":"Ownership and repeatability","body":["The commerce guide coordinates goods through four business areas; Brazil coordinates code through repositories, version sets and a build service. Different domains, the same instinct: make the boundary explicit and make the output repeatable.","The costs are similar too. Splitting a basket or a codebase into pieces buys independence but adds coordination at the seams.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"insight","text":"Where you place the split decides where the hard coordination lives."},"changes":["+ Explicit boundaries","+ Repeatable outputs","− Free coordination at the seams"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The pattern","lines":["commerce → split orders by warehouse","build    → split repositories by team","both     → define the seam, version the result"]}],"links":[]}},{"short":"Recap","chapter":"recap","title":"From supplier to snapshot","body":["Procurement brings goods in, inventory counts them, the e-commerce platform turns them into priced listings and takes an order, and transportation delivers it. Behind the screen, a system like Brazil turns each team’s declared config into a reproducible, versioned artifact.","The through-line: decide where one system hands off to the next, then make that hand-off boring and repeatable.","<span class=\"src\">Grounded in ByteByteGo’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/e-commerce-workflow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">E-commerce workflow</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/ByteByteGoHq/system-design-101/blob/main/data/guides/how-does-amazon-build-system-work.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Amazon’s build system: Brazil</a>.</span>"],"callout":{"kind":"next","text":"Next: <b>payment systems</b> — what changes when the thing moving is money."},"changes":["+ Four business areas","+ Reserve before payment","+ Version sets and DAG builds"],"scene":{"entities":[{"k":"p","type":"panel","x":0,"y":0,"title":"The rule","lines":["source    → suppliers and repositories","transform → inventory and DAG","commit    → orders and artifacts","deliver   → parcels and versions"]}],"links":[]}}]};
})();
